.TH TONETEST 1
.SH NAME
tonetest \- check that tone(2) synthesises what it claims
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tonetest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tonetest
plays through
.IR tone (2)'s
real code path and then reads back the samples it wrote, rather than
taking "it made a noise" as evidence.  It creates
.B /tmp/tonetest
holding an
.B audio
and an
.B audioctl
file and binds that directory over
.B /dev
with
.BR MBEFORE ,
so the library opens ordinary files and the PCM can be examined.  This
works whether or not the host has a sound device.
.PP
Three properties are checked.
.TP
.B a note has a shape
The
.B string
voice's first and last two hundred samples are compared against the
middle of the note.  A voice with an attack and a release is quiet at
both ends; a beeper is not.
.TP
.B a voice has a timbre
A Goertzel filter measures the energy at twice the fundamental of C5.
The
.B sine
voice must have almost none there and the
.B organ
voice must have a good deal.
.TP
.B parts mix
A single part playing C must contain almost no G; two parts playing C and
G together must contain both.  This is the property a one-bit square wave
cannot have at all.
.PP
.B -v
prints the measured numbers as well as the verdict.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tonetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tone (2),
.IR audio (3)
.SH BUGS
The checks are of kind rather than of quality: they establish that a note
is shaped, that a voice has harmonics and that parts mix, not that any of
it is in tune with an instrument or pleasant to listen to.
.PP
The captured files under
.B /tmp/tonetest
are left behind.
